ng mongod member context was resolved.'}\n\nResource percentages are contextual signals and do not by themselves establish saturation.\n\n## 8. Balancer and Sharding-Specific Metrics\n\n### Balancer\n\n${balancerValues.length\n  ? balancerValues.map(x =>\n      `- Enabled-state metric on ${x.node || x.service || 'mongos'}: last ${fmt(x.last)}.`\n    ).join('\\n')\n  : '- Balancer enabled-state metric was not detected.'}\n\n${balancerRunningValues.length\n  ? balancerRunningValues.map(x =>\n      `- Running-state snapshot on ${x.node || x.service || 'mongos'}: ${fmt(x.last)}.`\n    ).join('\\n')\n  : '- Balancer running-state metric was not detected.'}\n\n- Current mongos-reported shard count: ${shardingActivity.mongos_shards_total === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.mongos_shards_total)}.\n- Current mongos-reported draining-shard count: ${shardingActivity.mongos_shards_draining_total === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.mongos_shards_draining_total)}.\n\nBalancer enabled/running snapshots describe current state only. They do not prove migration activity, balancing effectiveness, or successful completion of chunk moves.\n\n### Seven-day observed move-chunk counter increases\n\n- Donor moveChunk started: ${shardingActivity.move_chunk_started_counter_increase_7d === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.move_chunk_started_counter_increase_7d)}.\n- Donor moveChunk committed: ${shardingActivity.move_chunk_committed_counter_increase_7d === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.move_chunk_committed_counter_increase_7d)}.\n- Donor moveChunk aborted: ${shardingActivity.move_chunk_aborted_counter_increase_7d === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.move_chunk_aborted_counter_increase_7d)}.\n\nThese are sums of observed member-local counter increases and are not asserted to equal a unique logical-migration count.\n\n### Range-deleter activity\n\n- Current range-deleter tasks - summed observed services: ${shardingActivity.range_deleter_tasks_current_sum === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.range_deleter_tasks_current_sum)}; highest member value: ${shardingActivity.range_deleter_tasks_current_max === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.range_deleter_tasks_current_max)}.\n- Documents deleted counter increase over 7 days: ${shardingActivity.range_deleter_docs_counter_increase_7d === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.range_deleter_docs_counter_increase_7d)}.\n- Bytes deleted counter increase over 7 days: ${shardingActivity.range_deleter_bytes_counter_increase_7d === null ? 'not available' : fmtBytes(shardingActivity.range_deleter_bytes_counter_increase_7d)}.\n- unfinishedMigrationFromPreviousPrimary - highest current observed value: ${shardingActivity.unfinished_migration_from_previous_primary_max === null ? 'not available' : fmt(shardingActivity.unfinished_migration_from_previous_primary_max)}.\n\nRange-deleter activity can be normal after chunk movement and is not by itself evidence of a problem.\n\nUnknown sharding-related PMM metrics remain inventory-only capability evidence in performance-safe mode.\n\n## 9. Risks and Analytical Limitations\n\n- Cluster assembly is mongos-anchored and uses shard names exposed by mongodb_shards_collection_chunks_count. PMM cluster=standalone and empty exporter cl_id are not used as authoritative boundaries.\n- A replica set is classified as a data shard only when PMM sharding metrics expose its name as a shard label.\n- If the shards collector is absent, the workflow does not guess which replica set is a data shard or config-server replica set.\n- No config-server service was explicitly identified in this execution; config-server replica-set health is therefore outside the observed cluster-health scope.\n- Chunk counts do not equal data size and do not prove data balance or shard-key quality.\n- dbStats raw dataSize/storageSize snapshots are database-level per-shard PMM-exported values; they can include data placement that is not equivalent to sharded-collection balance and are not filesystem-use measurements or growth trends.\n- Uneven shard PRIMARY workload does not by itself prove a bad shard key, hotspot cause, or routing problem.\n- mongos traffic does not by itself reveal scatter/gather targeting.\n- Balancer enabled/running state does not prove balancing effectiveness or migration success.\n- Move-chunk and range-deleter values are observed exported counters/state; they do not by themselves establish user impact.\n- clearJumboFlag command counters are not current jumbo-chunk state. Jumbo state is unavailable unless a dedicated state/count metric exists.\n- Query-level root cause still requires PMM QAN/profiler/explain or specific sharding-targeting metrics.\n- Unfamiliar dynamically discovered metric names remain inventory-only evidence rather than being assigned guessed semantics.\n\n## 10. Recommended DBA Follow-Up\n\n${(() => {\n  const actions = [];\n\n  if (!capabilities.shards_collector_chunk_metric) {\n    actions.push(\n      'If shard/chunk distribution analysis is required, verify whether the MongoDB exporter shards collector is enabled for the relevant PMM service(s).'\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (!capabilities.raw_shard_dbstats_data_size) {\n    actions.push(\n      'If current per-shard database footprint is required, verify whether dbStats collection is enabled in PMM.'\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (!configServerCoverageAvailable) {\n    actions.push(\n      'Verify whether the config-server replica-set members are registered and role-discoverable in PMM if full sharded-cluster health coverage is required. Do not infer config-server identity solely from replica sets that are absent from the shard list.'\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (resourceComparison.highest_filesystem_used_max) {\n    actions.push(\n      `Inspect the fullest filesystem on ${resourceComparison.highest_filesystem_used_max.node} (${fmt(resourceComparison.highest_filesystem_used_max.value)}%). Identify the actual mount/path and consumers before assigning a cause.`\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (worstLag) {\n    actions.push(\n      `Continue trending replication lag for ${worstLag.replica_set || 'the affected replica set'}, especially ${worstLagRows.map(x => x.observed_member).filter(Boolean).join(', ') || 'the affected member(s)'} where the maximum was ${fmt(worstLag.max)} sec. Investigate exact timestamps if lag becomes recurrent or materially elevated relative to baseline.`\n    );\n  }\n\n  if (\n    chunkLeader &&\n    dataLeader &&\n    readLeader &&\n    writeLeader &&\n    chunkLeader.shard === dataLeader.shard &&\n    dataLeader.shard === readLeader.shard &&\n    readLeader.shard === writeLeader.shard\n  ) {\n    actions.push(\n      `If the concentration on ${dataLeader.shard} is unexpected, review the affected sharded namespaces, shard-key/range and zone metadata, primary-shard placement of unsharded collections, and application access patterns before making a topology or shard-key change. PMM currently shows the same shard leading chunk count, dbStats dataSize, observed PRIMARY reads, and observed PRIMARY writes.`\n    );\n  } else if (shardPrimaryRows.length > 1) {\n    const sortedWrites = [...shardPrimaryRows]\n      .filter(x => finite(x.write_avg) !== null)\n      .sort((a,b) => b.write_avg - a.write_avg);\n\n    if (sortedWrites.length > 1 &&\n        sortedWrites[0].write_avg > 0 &&\n        sortedWrites[0].write_avg >= 3 * Math.max(sortedWrites[1].write_avg, 0.000001)) {\n      actions.push(\n        `Observed shard-PRIMARY write activity is concentrated on ${sortedWrites[0].shard}. If that distribution is unexpected, correlate it with shard metadata, chunk distribution and application behavior before drawing a shard-key conclusion.`\n      );\n    }\n  }\n\n  if (!actions.length) {\n    actions.push(\n      'No deterministic cluster-level follow-up was triggered beyond routine trend review.'\n    );\n  }\n\n  return actions.map((x,i) => `${i+1}. ${x}`).join('\\n');\n})()}\n`;\n\nconst compactMetricInventory = {\n  mongodb_metric_count:\n    Number(inventory.mongodb_metric_count || 0),\n\n  node_metric_count:\n    Number(inventory.node_metric_count || 0),\n\n  selected_query_count:\n    Number(inventory.selected_query_count || 0),\n\n  sharding_related_metric_count:\n    capabilities.discovered_sharding_metric_count,\n\n  dynamic_unmodeled_sharding_metric_count:\n    capabilities.dynamic_unmodeled_sharding_metric_count,\n\n  has_shards_collector_metric:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_shards_collector_metric),\n\n  has_balancer_metric:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_balancer_metric),\n\n  has_balancer_running_metric:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_balancer_running_metric),\n\n  has_raw_shard_dbstats_data_size:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_raw_shard_dbstats_data_size),\n\n  has_move_chunk_activity_metrics:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_move_chunk_activity_metrics),\n\n  has_range_deleter_activity_metrics:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_range_deleter_activity_metrics),\n\n  has_unfinished_migration_metric:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_unfinished_migration_metric),\n\n  has_jumbo_chunk_state_metric:\n    Boolean(inventory.has_jumbo_chunk_state_metric)\n};\n\nconst prompt = `You are a senior MongoDB database reliability engineer reviewing a PMM-monitored SHARDED CLUSTER.\n\nCORE PRINCIPLE:\nCHARACTERIZE AGGRESSIVELY; DIAGNOSE CONSERVATIVELY.\n\nEXECUTION ARCHITECTURE:\n- Raw seven-day time series are processed independently in replica-set, mongos, and cluster-metadata batches.\n- The cluster-level input contains compact statistical summaries, not one merged raw time-series payload.\n- Do not infer a cross-batch temporal correlation unless an explicitly supplied metric or event supports it.\n- A batch boundary is an execution/scalability boundary, not an operational isolation claim.\n\nThis workflow is PMM-METRICS-ONLY. Never claim information that is not present in the supplied capability matrix or metrics.\n\nDATA QUALITY:\n- The workflow intentionally limits seven-day query resolution with maxDataPoints. The data-quality gate already adjusts expected sampling for that limit.\n- Do not attribute reduced completeness to PMM agent/exporter connectivity, node load, or performance unless supplied error details explicitly show that.\n- If the gate is usable_with_caution because of a small query/extraction error count, identify it as partial metric-family coverage rather than declaring the entire analysis inaccurate.\n- Never invent the cause of a query/extraction error.\n\nSTRICT RULES:\n\n0. IDENTIFIER INTEGRITY\n- Copy node, service, replica-set, shard, and metric identifiers EXACTLY from the supplied JSON.\n- Never alter, normalize, duplicate, or invent hostname components.\n\n1. CLUSTER BOUNDARY\n- The workflow is mongos-anchored.\n- The selected mongos is the cluster anchor.\n- Data-shard replica sets are included only when mongodb_shards_collection_chunks_count from that mongos exposes their names in the shard label.\n- Additional mongos routers are included only when PMM exposes the same shard-set signature for them.\n- PMM cluster is NOT an authoritative boundary in this environment.\n- Exporter cl_id is NOT an authoritative boundary in this environment because it is empty.\n- If shard membership is unavailable, explicitly state that full cluster assembly is unavailable instead of guessing.\n\n2. TOPOLOGY\n- mongos is a router/service role, not a replica-set member state.\n- PRIMARY/SECONDARY/ARBITER come from replica-set member-state data.\n- Do not infer config-server identity unless the supplied PMM labels/metrics explicitly establish it.\n- If no config-server service is explicitly identified, state that config-server health is outside the observed topology coverage; do not silently treat the report as complete cluster-health coverage.\n- cl_role/cluster_role values equivalent to configsvr/config server are sufficient to identify a service as a config-server process role.\n- A replica set that is merely absent from the shard list is NOT automatically a config-server replica set.\n- One PRIMARY plus two SECONDARY members is a topology/state observation. NEVER call the replica set \"healthy\", \"fully healthy\", or \"operating normally\" from member-state composition alone.\n- If shard classification is unavailable, say so.\n\n3. DATA SHARDS\n- A replica set is treated as a data shard only when PMM sharding metrics expose its replica-set name as a shard label.\n- Never infer data-shard status from a name like \"shard0\" alone.\n\n4. CHUNKS / BALANCER / MIGRATION\n- Chunk count is not data size.\n- Uneven chunk counts do not automatically mean imbalance, bad shard key, or balancer failure.\n- dbStats raw dataSize/storageSize snapshots are current database-level per-shard statistics. They are not filesystem-use measurements, growth trends, or direct measurements of balance for only sharded collections.\n- If the same shard leads chunk count, dbStats dataSize, observed PRIMARY reads, and observed PRIMARY writes, describe this as strong or multi-signal concentration.\n- Prefer \"concentration\" or \"asymmetry\" over \"imbalance\" unless an expected/desired distribution is explicitly supplied.\n- Do not assign a cause without shard metadata and application/query evidence.\n- Balancer enabled != balancer currently running != migration activity != successful balancing.\n- A current balancer-running value describes the sampled instant only.\n- If modeled moveChunk counters show zero increase, say exactly that. NEVER infer that the balancer is \"not addressing\" the distribution, \"not progressing\", \"stuck\", \"failing\", or \"inactive\". The reason is not established by these metrics.\n- moveChunk started/committed/aborted values are sums of observed member-local seven-day counter increases; do not call them exact unique migration counts.\n- Range-deleter activity can be normal cleanup after migration and is not inherently a problem.\n- clearJumboFlag command counters do NOT establish current jumbo chunks.\n- Claim jumbo-chunk state only if a dedicated jumbo state/count metric is explicitly supplied.\n- If a modeled migration/jumbo/range-deletion signal is absent, say that dimension is unavailable.\n\n5. SHARD WORKLOAD\n- Compare workload across shard PRIMARY members.\n- Do not call the sum exact application traffic.\n- Observed concentration on one shard is a workload-distribution fact, not proof of a bad shard key or hot-shard cause.\n- You may state that workload share, chunk-count share, and dbStats data-size share are simultaneously concentrated on the same shard when the supplied values show that. This is a multi-signal observation, not a causal explanation.\n- Never say \"bad shard key\" without shard metadata plus query/data evidence.\n\n6. MONGOS\n- Compare routers individually.\n- Similar mongos counters support only: \"observed mongos activity is similar across the monitored routers.\"\n- NEVER infer that client applications are distributing traffic evenly, that load balancing is configured correctly, or that connections/requests are intentionally balanced from similar counters.\n- Do not infer scatter/gather, targeting efficiency, readPreference, or routing policy from generic read/write/command/network counters.\n- If targeting-specific metrics are not supplied, mark targeting efficiency unavailable.\n\n7. REPLICATION / OPLOG\n- Keep replica-set observations role-aware.\n- No universal lag threshold.\n- NEVER call lag \"low\", \"high\", \"acceptable\", \"problematic\", or say it \"warrants attention\" solely from the numeric value.\n- Keep P99 and max distinct. NEVER write phrases such as \"P99 max lag\".\n- Do not explain lag spikes as write bursts, secondary struggle, disk pressure, or performance issues unless the same-event evidence explicitly supports that.\n- Delayed secondaries require configuration context.\n- Oplog window is replication/catch-up context only.\n- NEVER call an oplog window unusually/exceptionally large or small, sufficient/insufficient for recovery, or evidence of incorrect oplog sizing without explicit baseline/configuration/recovery requirements.\n- If useful, compare worst lag to oplog window numerically without qualitative headroom language.\n- Temporal alignment does not prove causality.\n\n8. DATA SIZE\n- Only analyze data-size/index-size/storage-size distribution when dbStats/collStats metrics actually exist and the metric semantics are explicit.\n- Never use chunk counts as a proxy for bytes.\n\n9. RAW DYNAMIC PMM METRICS\n- Raw dynamically discovered sharding metrics are evidence of capability.\n- Do not invent counter/gauge semantics, rates, thresholds, or causes for an unfamiliar raw metric.\n- You may list them in the capability/limitations section.\n\n10. HOST / WIREDTIGER\n- No universal CPU/memory/disk/WT threshold is supplied.\n- Resource differences are member context, not proof of saturation.\n- NEVER call CPU, memory, disk busy, or WiredTiger occupancy high/low/stable/healthy solely from percentages.\n- WiredTiger cache occupancy around 80% does NOT prove a large working set, cache pressure, or that cache sizing should be changed.\n- Dirty-cache percentage alone does not establish write pressure.\n- disk busy is busy-time percentage, not throughput, IOPS, latency, or generic \"disk I/O activity\".\n- Do not explain higher disk busy as replication writes, secondary reads, or another cause without direct evidence.\n- Do not say resource values \"directly correlate\" with workload/data concentration. You may say they are higher on the same shard, without causal/correlation claims.\n\n11. RECOMMENDATIONS\n- Evidence-driven only.\n- If config-server topology is absent, recommend verifying whether config-server members are registered and role-discoverable in PMM. Do NOT automatically say \"enable config-server monitoring\".\n- Do not call missing config-server coverage a \"critical risk\"; it is a coverage limitation for a full cluster-health review.\n- Do not recommend adding shards, changing shard keys, changing zones, resizing WiredTiger cache/RAM, or changing balancer configuration from PMM summary metrics alone.\n- Do not invent MongoDB shell helpers or commands. Prefer reviewing shard-key definitions, ranges/chunks, zones, collection balancing settings, primary-shard placement of unsharded data, balancer configuration/logs, QAN, profiler, and explain output.\n- Do not speculate that filesystem usage is temporary files, logs, or non-MongoDB data. Identify the mount/path and consumers first.\n- Query root cause needs PMM QAN/profiler/explain.\n- Shard-key/routing diagnosis needs sharding metadata and application/query context.\n\nREQUIRED STRUCTURE:\n\n# PMM MongoDB Sharded Cluster 7-Day Operational Review\n## Cluster Overview\n## Technical Assessment\n## 1. PMM Sharding Capability Matrix\n## 2. Topology: mongos and Replica Sets\n## 3. Data Shard and Chunk Distribution\n## 4. Shard PRIMARY Workload Distribution\n## 5. Replica Set Health and Replication\n## 6. mongos Workload, Connections, and Network\n## 7. WiredTiger and Member Resource Context\n## 8. Balancer and Sharding-Specific Metrics\n## 9. Risks and Analytical Limitations\n## 10. Recommended DBA Follow-Up\n\nIf a section is unsupported by currently exposed PMM metrics, explicitly state that instead of filling it with generic MongoDB knowledge.\n\nInput JSON:\n${JSON.stringify({\n  report_type: 'MongoDB sharded-cluster PMM operational analysis',\n  cluster_profile: clusterProfile,\n  data_quality_gate: analysis.data_quality_gate,\n  metric_inventory: compactMetricInventory,\n  requested_period: analysis.requested_period\n})}\n`;\n\nreturn [{\n  json: {\n    report_input: {\n      cluster_profile: clusterProfile,\n      data_quality_gate: analysis.data_quality_gate,\n      metric_inventory: compactMetricInventory,\n      requested_period: analysis.requested_period\n    },\n    prompt,\n    fallback_markdown: fallbackMarkdown,\n    prompt_characters: prompt.length,\n    metric_count_sent_to_model: metrics.length,\n    cluster_profile_generated: true\n  }\n}];"
